  <p class="text-lg mb-10">
    Our system is designed to work with democratic institutions such as parliament
    or city halls in mind. To reflect this, we consider the existence of an
    administrative council (AC), each member of which represents a political party
    present in the parliament or city hall.
    <br \> <br \>
    Whenever a member of this administrative council wishes to perform an action in
    order to add, delete, or read, or otherwise modify a resource that belongs to all
    of the parliament or city hall, they draft the code they wish to execute and then
    submit it to all other administrators on the AC.
    <br \><br \>
    However, we wished to ensure that different parliaments or city halls can easily
    integrate ParSec into their existing infrastructure.
    <br \><br \>
    To achieve this, we developed a new access control system called <b>Joint User
    Do (Judo)</b>. Judo is an access control mechanism built around two mandatory
    components: the Judo-Daemon and Voters. The Judo-Daemon exposes three API
    commands: Authorize, Execute, and Audit. Both Authorize and Execute accept a
    command as input. Authorize performs a dry run, returning whether the command would
    be approved, while Execute performs the same authorization check and, if approved,
    carries it out. End users interact with the daemon either through a PAM module or
    through an application-level frontend process.
    <br \><br \>
    To determine whether a command should be approved, the Judo-Daemon forwards it to
    the Voters. Each Voter requests manual input from a designated user, who decides
    whether the command should proceed. If a sufficient majority of Voters approve the
    command, it is authorized. The privacy of individual votes is enforced by a Secret
    Sharing-based Secure Multiparty Computation protocol: upon completion, the Judo-Daemon
    learns only whether a majority was reached, and each Voter learns only its own user's
    vote. Critically, these guarantees hold even if some Voter instances behave maliciously
    and deviate from the protocol.
    <br \><br \>
    To maintain accountability, the Audit command enables controlled disclosure of individual
    votes for a previously submitted command. When the daemon receives an Audit request
    referencing a prior Authorize or Execute call, for instance, when a user disputes the
    recorded outcome, the Voters collectively decide whether to disclose all votes cast for
    that command.
    <br \>
</Layout>
